<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <groupId>com.github.LiQiyeDev</groupId>
    <artifactId>botmaker-sdk</artifactId>
    <version>0.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>

    <properties>
        <maven.compiler.release>25</maven.compiler.release>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
        <!-- Stamped into the jar manifest (Build-Time) so a bot/editor can tell which SDK build it loaded;
             crucial for local dev where every rebuild reuses the same 0.0.0-SNAPSHOT jar file name. -->
        <maven.build.timestamp.format>yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss'Z'</maven.build.timestamp.format>
        <!-- botmaker-shared version. Committed value is ALWAYS 0.0.0-SNAPSHOT: it resolves the sibling
             module in the umbrella reactor / dev-install. The real released shared tag is injected only at
             build time by CI (jitpack.yml) via -Dbotmaker.shared.version=<tag> — never committed here, so a
             clean checkout matches the reactor/dev value and there is no stale pin to get out of sync.

             That injection only reaches CONSUMERS because of flatten-maven-plugin below. Maven installs and
             publishes the COMMITTED pom, not the effective one, so a -D on the command line changes what
             this build resolves and nothing about what a bot resolving this SDK from JitPack sees. Without
             flatten, the published pom still said botmaker-shared:${botmaker.shared.version} with the
             property at 0.0.0-SNAPSHOT, and every released SDK failed to resolve on any machine that had
             never built this repo locally. Do not remove the plugin without replacing the mechanism. -->
        <botmaker.shared.version>0.0.0-SNAPSHOT</botmaker.shared.version>
        <!-- botmaker-session version. Same contract as botmaker.shared.version above: always 0.0.0-SNAPSHOT
             in the committed pom, injected at build time by CI for a released tag, resolved into the
             published pom by flatten. -->
        <botmaker.session.version>0.0.0-SNAPSHOT</botmaker.session.version>
        <!-- botmaker-studio-api version: the Studio plugin contract. Same 0.0.0-SNAPSHOT contract again.
             This one moves far more slowly than the other two on purpose — see the dependency below. -->
        <botmaker.studioapi.version>0.0.0-SNAPSHOT</botmaker.studioapi.version>
        <!-- botmaker-plugin-toolkit version: the widgets this module's slot editors are built from. Same
             0.0.0-SNAPSHOT contract as the three above, and the same flatten reason — it IS in
             <dependencies>, so the published pom must carry a resolvable version.

             NOT optional, and this comment said it was until 2026-09-06, three days after the element
             below stopped being: `optional` means not transitive, so a host resolving botmaker-sdk got a
             jar whose SdkPlugin could not resolve its own superclass. See the dependency's own comment. -->
        <botmaker.plugintoolkit.version>0.0.0-SNAPSHOT</botmaker.plugintoolkit.version>
        <!-- botmaker-plugin-basics version: PLUGIN #2, which owns the nine JDK value types this module's
             WireText delegates the parsing of. Same 0.0.0-SNAPSHOT contract as the four above and the same
             flatten reason — it is an ordinary <dependencies> entry, so the published pom must carry a
             resolvable version, and cutting plugin-basics therefore forces an SDK release. -->
        <botmaker.pluginbasics.version>0.0.0-SNAPSHOT</botmaker.pluginbasics.version>
        <!-- The published tag japicmp compares this build against. Bump it in the release commit, beside the
             CHANGELOG section, so it is always the LAST release — the gate then asks "did this build remove
             anything the previous one offered?", which is the whole of the never-delete rule.

             THE LAST PRE-POLICY REMOVAL IS IN v1.2.0 ITSELF (2026-09-11): `api.config.Settings` and
             `api.config.Wire` are deleted, not deprecated — the maintainer's explicit call, recorded as
             decision 8 of the settings-becomes-a-plugin plan. That is safe against this gate for exactly one
             reason, and it is the reason the baseline is what it is: the baseline is the release that
             CONTAINS the deletion, so the jar the gate compares against already lacks both classes and
             never-delete begins from a surface that no longer offers them. Nothing here is an exemption, an
             ignore list or a skip — the rule stays unconditional, it simply starts one release later than
             those two classes ended. The cost is stated where a user meets it: a bot importing either class
             stops compiling, with no migrator and only a readable error naming the type.

             It is v1.2.0, the release never-delete BEGINS at, and not the currently-newest tag v1.1.0. The
             rule is not retroactive and cannot be: v1.1.0 → now already removed api.config.Wire, @Palette,
             @Scaffolding and Text's nine shared-OcrOptions overloads, every one of them a deliberate decision
             recorded in CLAUDE.md and taken while api.* was still freely breakable. Pointing the baseline at
             v1.1.0 would demand they all come back. Until v1.2.0 is tagged the baseline does not resolve, and
             ignoreMissingOldVersion below reports that rather than failing a build for a reason nobody can
             act on — so the gate arms itself the moment the release it guards from exists.

             It is resolved from JitPack, so an OFFLINE build that has never fetched it must pass
             -Dbotmaker.japicmp.skip=true. The gate is otherwise unconditional: under never-delete there is
             no legitimate removal, and so nothing to allow. -->
        <botmaker.japicmp.baseline>v1.2.0</botmaker.japicmp.baseline>
        <botmaker.japicmp.skip>false</botmaker.japicmp.skip>
        <!-- JavaFX, for the slot editors only. Held in one place because the two artifacts must move
             together — javafx-controls is compiled against one exact javafx-graphics. -->
        <javafx.version>25.0.4</javafx.version>

        <!--
          The OCR stack's three versions move TOGETHER. Tess4J pins a lept4j, and lept4j's generated
          bindings target one exact Leptonica release; the JavaCPP natives below must be that release
          and the Tesseract that Tess4J was built against.

          tess4j 5.19.0 -> lept4j 1.24.0 -> Leptonica 1.87.0 (its Windows DLL is libleptonica1870.dll)
                        -> Tesseract 5.5.x            (its Windows DLL is libtesseract552.dll)

          Getting this wrong does NOT fail the build. It fails at the first OcrEngine.recognize() call,
          in a user's bot, as an undefined-symbol UnsatisfiedLinkError out of Leptonica1's static
          initialiser (measured: Leptonica 1.85.0 under lept4j 1.24.0 dies on pixFindBaselinesGen).
          OcrEngineNativeTest is the guard — it calls getWords(), the only path that reaches these
          bindings. When bumping Tess4J, read the new lept4j's bundled DLL name to find the Leptonica
          version to match, and check that org.bytedeco publishes it.

          This block and everything it pins moved here from botmaker-shared/pom.xml in SDK 1.2.0, with
          com.botmaker.shared.ocr -> com.botmaker.sdk.{api.vision,internal.ocr}. It is reproduced
          VERBATIM on purpose: the failure it describes is a user's, at runtime, and nothing in any
          build would have caught the paraphrase.
        -->
        <tess4j.version>5.19.0</tess4j.version>
        <bytedeco.tesseract.version>5.5.3-1.5.14</bytedeco.tesseract.version>
        <bytedeco.leptonica.version>1.87.0-1.5.14</bytedeco.leptonica.version>
    </properties>

    <repositories>
        <!-- JitPack: resolves com.github.LiQiyeDev:botmaker-shared:<tag> when this SDK is built
             standalone against a released shared (the umbrella reactor resolves the SNAPSHOT locally
             instead, and JitPack's own build env injects this repo too). -->
        <repository>
            <id>jitpack.io</id>
            <url>https://jitpack.io</url>
        </repository>
    </repositories>

    <dependencies>
        <!-- Shared cross-platform native window plumbing (enumerate / capture / focus / input).
             com.github.LiQiyeDev coordinate + version property so this resolves from the umbrella
             reactor locally and from JitPack when published (see the property comment above). -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.github.LiQiyeDev</groupId>
            <artifactId>botmaker-shared</artifactId>
            <version>${botmaker.shared.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Private display sessions (com.botmaker.session.*): the nested :N display a bot isolates into,
             reached from Target.start() via internal.session.SessionBootstrap. Same coordinate/property
             contract as shared above. It depends on shared itself, so this is additive, not a replacement. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.github.LiQiyeDev</groupId>
            <artifactId>botmaker-session</artifactId>
            <version>${botmaker.session.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- The Studio plugin contract, at `compile`: a bot's own code carries @Param and @Managed, so it
             needs the contract jar to compile. PluginLoader is parent-first for com.botmaker.plugin.api.**,
             so a plugin still links the host's copy. Nothing may declare the contract directly beside this
             SDK in a bot's pom (nearest-wins would pin a version this jar was not built against). -->

        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.github.LiQiyeDev</groupId>
            <artifactId>botmaker-studio-api</artifactId>
            <version>${botmaker.studioapi.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- The plugin toolkit, at plain `compile` and never `optional`: PluginLoader is child-first for it,
             so a plugin brings its own, and `optional` (not transitive) would leave SdkPlugin unloadable in a
             project that adds the SDK. It has no dependencies, so a bot pays only its own jar. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.github.LiQiyeDev</groupId>
            <artifactId>botmaker-plugin-toolkit</artifactId>
            <version>${botmaker.plugintoolkit.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Plugin #2 (the JDK value types, Settings, PluginData), at `compile`: an ordinary plugin-to-plugin
             dependency. Nothing may declare it directly beside this SDK in a bot's pom (nearest-wins). -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.github.LiQiyeDev</groupId>
            <artifactId>botmaker-plugin-basics</artifactId>
            <version>${botmaker.pluginbasics.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- JavaFX, Javalin and ZXing are `optional`: only the plugin half (Studio-side) links them, and a
             headless bot must resolve none of them. javafx.* is parent-first, so the host supplies it. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.openjfx</groupId>
            <artifactId>javafx-controls</artifactId>
            <version>${javafx.version}</version>
            <optional>true</optional>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.openjfx</groupId>
            <artifactId>javafx-graphics</artifactId>
            <version>${javafx.version}</version>
            <optional>true</optional>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Javalin: the Remote Pilot's server (static client, routes, WebSocket). -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.javalin</groupId>
            <artifactId>javalin</artifactId>
            <version>6.7.0</version>
            <optional>true</optional>
        </dependency>

        <!-- ZXing core: the pilot's pairing QR codes, drawn straight into a JavaFX WritableImage. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.google.zxing</groupId>
            <artifactId>core</artifactId>
            <version>3.5.3</version>
            <optional>true</optional>
        </dependency>

        <!-- JavaCV / OpenCV (native bindings used for image matching) -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.openpnp</groupId>
            <artifactId>opencv</artifactId>
            <version>4.9.0-0</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Tess4J: Tesseract behind internal.ocr and api.vision.Text. Windows natives are bundled; Linux
             ones are staged by <build>. Pinned together with lept4j/bytedeco — see <properties>. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>net.sourceforge.tess4j</groupId>
            <artifactId>tess4j</artifactId>
            <version>${tess4j.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- JNA (native Windows/Linux window + input control) -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>net.java.dev.jna</groupId>
            <artifactId>jna</artifactId>
            <version>5.14.0</version>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>net.java.dev.jna</groupId>
            <artifactId>jna-platform</artifactId>
            <version>5.14.0</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Jackson Databind (config serialization) -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.core</groupId>
            <artifactId>jackson-databind</artifactId>
            <version>2.17.0</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Test Dependencies -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter-api</artifactId>
            <version>5.10.2</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter-engine</artifactId>
            <version>5.10.2</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.platform</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-platform-launcher</artifactId>
            <version>1.10.2</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!-- ClassGraph, test only: ApiPointersTest reads @ReplacedBy out of target/classes. Same version as
             Studio, which reads the same annotations out of a resolved SDK jar. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.github.classgraph</groupId>
            <artifactId>classgraph</artifactId>
            <version>4.8.179</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <!-- No <resources> block: Maven's default (src/main/resources) is what this module wants.

             There was one for a day, copying com/botmaker/sdk/internal/plugin/seeds/*.java into the jar so a
             host could read a seed's TEXT back out with getResourceAsStream. The seeds are deleted and so is
             the surface that read them — a plugin does not write files into a user's project. Declaring
             <resources> at all switches off the default and means re-declaring it, so leaving the block out
             is not merely tidier: it is one less thing that silently drops src/main/resources if edited
             carelessly. -->
        <plugins>
            <!-- Maven Compiler Plugin — no annotation processor at all, since 2026-08-27.

                 There were two passes, then one, and now none. src/apt/java held ApiPointerProcessor and
                 PaletteCatalogProcessor; both moved into botmaker-plugin-processor as one
                 PluginSurfaceProcessor named here in <annotationProcessorPaths>; and then the catalog stopped
                 being generated. SdkPlugin builds it with PaletteCatalog.of(...) instead, reflecting the
                 facades at load time.

                 The property the generator was defended on — "a catalog naming a renamed member does not
                 compile" — does not need saving, because members are now DISCOVERED rather than named:
                 @Hidden, @PaletteDefault and @PaletteLabel travel with the member they annotate, so there is
                 nothing left to go stale. What stays javac-checked is the class list, which is written as
                 class literals in SdkPlugin.

                 It also cost something a third-party plugin could not pay: a pom that omitted this block got
                 no catalog and nothing said why. Reflection needs no build configuration to be right. -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.11.0</version>
                <configuration>
                    <!-- Emit the MethodParameters attribute so real parameter names survive in the
                         bytecode. Studio (which does NOT depend on the SDK) reads them from the resolved
                         SDK jar via ClassGraph to label call arguments (e.g. findCompare(good, bad))
                         instead of arg0/arg1. -->
                    <parameters>true</parameters>
                    <proc>none</proc>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>

            <!--
              Stage the Linux OCR natives into the jar, under JNA's Platform.RESOURCE_PREFIX for this
              platform (linux-x86-64). Tess4J's LoadLibs static initialiser extracts every classpath
              resource under that prefix to a temp dir and prepends it to jna.library.path — the same
              mechanism that already makes Windows self-contained through win32-x86-64/. So this needs
              no Java code: put the files on the classpath and Tess4J finds them.

              WHY WE SHIP THEM AT ALL: Tess4J publishes Windows DLLs only. On Linux it dlopen()s the
              system libtesseract, so OCR failed with UnsatisfiedLinkError anywhere it wasn't installed —
              the AppImage, the tarball and every generated bot, none of which have a package manager to
              declare a dependency to.

              WHY THE JAVACPP PRESETS: Fedora's libtesseract links libcurl, so its ldd closure is 46
              libraries / 29 MB (openssl, krb5, ldap, sasl, ssh — none of which OCR uses). The bytedeco
              build needs only libleptonica.so.6, libstdc++, libm, libgcc_s and libc, with the image
              codecs statically linked in, and carries RUNPATH $ORIGIN/ so tesseract finds leptonica
              beside itself. Two files, 9.4 MB, no other system library beyond what a JVM already needs.

              THE TWO FILENAMES ARE LOAD-BEARING, both established by testing:
                - Tesseract is renamed to the UNVERSIONED libtesseract.so. Left as libtesseract.so.5.5,
                  JNA's lookup skipped it and loaded the system /usr/lib64 copy instead, which silently
                  defeats the whole point on any machine that has one.
                - Leptonica KEEPS libleptonica.so.6, because that is the literal NEEDED string inside
                  libtesseract.so. Adding an unversioned copy alongside it makes the process map
                  leptonica twice.

              The bytedeco jars also carry headers and .a files (~2 MB); the includes below drop them.

              This moved here from botmaker-shared/pom.xml in SDK 1.2.0, unchanged apart from living
              beside the code it serves. The staging half is the maven-antrun execution below.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-dependency-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.6.1</version>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>unpack-linux-ocr-natives</id>
                        <phase>generate-resources</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>unpack</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <artifactItems>
                                <artifactItem>
                                    <groupId>org.bytedeco</groupId>
                                    <artifactId>tesseract</artifactId>
                                    <version>${bytedeco.tesseract.version}</version>
                                    <classifier>linux-x86_64</classifier>
                                    <includes>**/libtesseract.so.*</includes>
                                </artifactItem>
                                <artifactItem>
                                    <groupId>org.bytedeco</groupId>
                                    <artifactId>leptonica</artifactId>
                                    <version>${bytedeco.leptonica.version}</version>
                                    <classifier>linux-x86_64</classifier>
                                    <includes>**/libleptonica.so.*</includes>
                                </artifactItem>
                            </artifactItems>
                            <outputDirectory>${project.build.directory}/native-stage</outputDirectory>
                            <overWriteReleases>false</overWriteReleases>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>

            <!-- Antrun: TWO executions, unrelated to each other beyond sharing a plugin. `whats-new`
                 copies the changelog into the jar; `stage-linux-ocr-natives` finishes the OCR native
                 staging the dependency plugin above started. They are one <plugin> because Maven takes a
                 single declaration per plugin per pom, not because they belong together.

                 whats-new: put this module's CHANGELOG.md inside the jar, as
                 META-INF/botmaker/whats-new.md.

                 Studio's Project > Upgrade SDK... leads with what a release GIVES you, before it lists
                 what it costs. It reads that out of the target jar it has already downloaded to diff, so
                 the answer is offline and needs no GitHub API, no release notes and no network at all.

                 The WHOLE file, not the section being upgraded to: a bot may jump several releases at
                 once, so the jar has to be able to answer every span ending at its own version. A jar
                 built from a tree with no changelog simply has no entry, and Studio falls back to the API
                 diff it showed before — the standing rule that every new reader degrades.

                 A copy rather than <resource> on the module root: the root is not a resource directory
                 and making it one would sweep the pom, the LICENSE and .deps.env into the jar too. And
                 antrun rather than maven-resources-plugin because the file has to be RENAMED — the entry
                 name is a contract with Studio's reader and must not follow whatever the file is called
                 in the repo — which copy-resources cannot do (it has no fileMappers on that goal).

                 tolerant of a missing source: `failonerror=false` keeps a tree with no changelog building,
                 which is what a jar built from an older tag is. -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-antrun-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.1.0</version>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>whats-new</id>
                        <phase>process-resources</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>run</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <target>
                                <copy file="${project.basedir}/CHANGELOG.md"
                                      tofile="${project.build.outputDirectory}/META-INF/botmaker/whats-new.md"
                                      failonerror="false" overwrite="true"/>
                            </target>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                    <execution>
                        <id>stage-linux-ocr-natives</id>
                        <phase>process-resources</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>run</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <target>
                                <property name="ocr.natives.dir"
                                          value="${project.build.outputDirectory}/linux-x86-64"/>
                                <mkdir dir="${ocr.natives.dir}"/>
                                <!-- Flattened and renamed: see the filename note above. -->
                                <copy todir="${ocr.natives.dir}" flatten="true" failonerror="true">
                                    <fileset dir="${project.build.directory}/native-stage"
                                             includes="**/libtesseract.so.*"/>
                                    <mapper type="merge" to="libtesseract.so"/>
                                </copy>
                                <copy todir="${ocr.natives.dir}" flatten="true" failonerror="true">
                                    <fileset dir="${project.build.directory}/native-stage"
                                             includes="**/libleptonica.so.*"/>
                                </copy>
                            </target>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>

            <!-- Jar Plugin: stamp the manifest with Implementation-* (pom version/vendor) and a Build-Time
                 so a running bot / Studio's editor can report exactly which SDK build was loaded. The pom
                 version is cosmetic (0.0.0-SNAPSHOT for local dev; JitPack overrides it with the tag), so
                 Build-Time is the signal that distinguishes local rebuilds sharing one jar file name. -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-jar-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.3.0</version>
                <configuration>
                    <archive>
                        <manifest>
                            <addDefaultImplementationEntries>true</addDefaultImplementationEntries>
                        </manifest>
                        <manifestEntries>
                            <Build-Time>${maven.build.timestamp}</Build-Time>
                        </manifestEntries>
                    </archive>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>

            <!-- Flatten Plugin: publish a pom whose ${...} are already resolved.

                 This is what makes the botmaker.shared.version / botmaker.session.version contract above
                 actually work. `mvn install -Dbotmaker.shared.version=v0.0.18` installs the COMMITTED pom
                 (still carrying the property at 0.0.0-SNAPSHOT), so the injected tag never reached a
                 consumer: a generated bot pinning a released SDK failed with "Could not find artifact
                 com.github.LiQiyeDev:botmaker-shared:jar:0.0.0-SNAPSHOT in jitpack.io" on any machine with
                 no local build in ~/.m2. Flatten substitutes the effective values into
                 target/.flattened-pom.xml and installs THAT, so the published pom names the real tag.

                 flattenMode=oss keeps dependencies/licenses/scm and drops build+profiles (neither is read
                 by a consumer here — this module has no profiles; do not copy this to botmaker-shared,
                 whose OS-activated native profiles a consumer DOES need). It also strips repositories,
                 hence the explicit keep: the jitpack.io repo declared above is how a consumer reaches the
                 transitive shared/session at all. The clean execution removes the generated file. -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.codehaus.mojo</groupId>
                <artifactId>flatten-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <!-- Held at 1.4.1 for JitPack, NOT stale: 1.5.0 declares a Maven 3.5.4 prerequisite and
                     1.6.0 declares 3.6.3, and JitPack's build image runs older than 3.6.3 — v0.0.7/v1.0.25
                     were burned finding that out ("requires Maven version 3.6.3", build failed before it
                     published anything). 1.4.1 needs only 3.2.5 and supports the same flattenMode and
                     pomElements config used here. Do not bump without proving JitPack's mvn version. -->
                <version>1.4.1</version>
                <configuration>
                    <flattenMode>oss</flattenMode>
                    <pomElements>
                        <repositories>keep</repositories>
                    </pomElements>
                </configuration>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>flatten</id>
                        <phase>process-resources</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>flatten</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>
                    <execution>
                        <id>flatten.clean</id>
                        <phase>clean</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>clean</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>

            <!-- Source Plugin: attaches source code so consumers get Javadoc/code in their IDE -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-source-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.3.0</version>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>attach-sources</id>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>jar-no-fork</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.jetbrains.dokka</groupId>
                <artifactId>dokka-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>1.9.20</version> <!-- Latest stable 1.9.x version -->
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <phase>pre-site</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>dokka</goal> <!-- 'dokka' generates the modern HTML UI -->
                        </goals>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
                <configuration>
                    <sourceDirectories>
                        <dir>${project.basedir}/src/main/java</dir>
                    </sourceDirectories>

                    <!-- Optional: Add a custom home page (like a Swagger overview) -->
                    <!--
                    <includes>
                        <include>${project.basedir}/README.md</include>
                    </includes>
                    -->
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

    <profiles>
        <!-- The API gate, in a profile so that a build which only wants the ARTIFACT can decline to load
             the plugin at all.

             ACTIVE BY DEFAULT: the activation is the ABSENCE of botmaker.japicmp.skip, and a property
             declared in <properties> above does not count for profile activation — only a -D does. So
             `mvn verify` runs the gate exactly as before, and only an explicit
             `-Dbotmaker.japicmp.skip=true` turns it off. It is a property activation rather than
             <activeByDefault> for a second reason: activeByDefault is cancelled the moment any profile is
             named on the command line, so `mvn -Ppilot package` would silently drop the gate.

             WHY THE PROFILE RATHER THAN THE <skip> FLAG: skipping is a decision the mojo makes after Maven
             has resolved and loaded the plugin, and this plugin declares a PREREQUISITE. JitPack's builder
             runs an older Maven, and botmaker-studio-api v0.0.1 died there before any configuration was
             read — `The plugin ... requires Maven version 3.6.3` — publishing no artifact, which took
             botmaker-sdk v1.1.1 down with it. A gate that can only refuse a tag ALREADY PUSHED protects
             nothing and can only break the publish; the refusal that matters happens in CI, before the tag
             exists. Same reasoning as the `-DskipTests` jitpack.yml has always passed. -->
        <profile>
            <id>api-gate</id>
            <activation>
                <property>
                    <name>!botmaker.japicmp.skip</name>
                </property>
            </activation>
            <build>
                <plugins>
                    <!-- japicmp — com.botmaker.sdk.api.** never removes anything.

                         This is what makes dropping @Replaces safe. The back edge existed because Studio
                         holds only two jars at upgrade time — the bot's pin and the target — so a bot on 1.0
                         jumping to 3.0 could not see a pointer added in 2.0 on an element deleted in 3.0.
                         Under never-delete the target jar still carries the deprecated element AND its own
                         @ReplacedBy, so the forward pointer alone answers every upgrade including a skipped
                         one. A rename is: add the new name, deprecate the old, keep both, point one at the
                         other.

                         The 2026-08-22 gate died because CI could not tell an intended break from an
                         accident — it cannot see the version, and the SDK was allowed to break in a major.
                         This rule is UNCONDITIONAL, so that objection does not apply: there is no legitimate
                         removal to distinguish. Hence no ignore list, no exemption annotation and no verdict
                         file — the escape hatch is what killed the last one. No postAnalysisScript either:
                         its bundled Groovy cannot read Java 26 class files.

                         Scoped to api.** on purpose. internal.** stays freely breakable, as it must. -->
                    <plugin>
                        <groupId>com.github.siom79.japicmp</groupId>
                        <artifactId>japicmp-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                        <version>0.23.1</version>
                        <executions>
                            <execution>
                                <phase>verify</phase>
                                <goals>
                                    <goal>cmp</goal>
                                </goals>
                            </execution>
                        </executions>
                        <configuration>
                            <oldVersion>
                                <dependency>
                                    <groupId>com.github.LiQiyeDev</groupId>
                                    <artifactId>botmaker-sdk</artifactId>
                                    <version>${botmaker.japicmp.baseline}</version>
                                    <type>jar</type>
                                </dependency>
                            </oldVersion>
                            <parameter>
                                <onlyModified>true</onlyModified>
                                <!-- The baseline is the release never-delete begins at, which until it is
                                     tagged does not exist; a baseline that cannot be fetched reports rather
                                     than failing a build for a reason the author cannot act on. See the
                                     property comment. -->
                                <ignoreMissingOldVersion>true</ignoreMissingOldVersion>
                                <includes>
                                    <include>com.botmaker.sdk.api</include>
                                </includes>
                                <breakBuildBasedOnSemanticVersioning>false</breakBuildBasedOnSemanticVersioning>
                                <!-- Binary/source incompatibility in general is NOT the rule — only removal
                                     is, and the override block below is what makes exactly that fatal. -->
                                <breakBuildOnBinaryIncompatibleModifications>true</breakBuildOnBinaryIncompatibleModifications>
                                <breakBuildOnSourceIncompatibleModifications>true</breakBuildOnSourceIncompatibleModifications>
                                <includeSynthetic>false</includeSynthetic>
                                <ignoreMissingClasses>true</ignoreMissingClasses>
                                <!-- The whole rule: nothing under api.** is ever removed. -->
                                <overrideCompatibilityChangeParameters>
                                    <overrideCompatibilityChangeParameter>
                                        <compatibilityChange>METHOD_REMOVED</compatibilityChange>
                                        <binaryCompatible>false</binaryCompatible>
                                        <sourceCompatible>false</sourceCompatible>
                                    </overrideCompatibilityChangeParameter>
                                    <overrideCompatibilityChangeParameter>
                                        <compatibilityChange>CLASS_REMOVED</compatibilityChange>
                                        <binaryCompatible>false</binaryCompatible>
                                        <sourceCompatible>false</sourceCompatible>
                                    </overrideCompatibilityChangeParameter>
                                    <overrideCompatibilityChangeParameter>
                                        <compatibilityChange>FIELD_REMOVED</compatibilityChange>
                                        <binaryCompatible>false</binaryCompatible>
                                        <sourceCompatible>false</sourceCompatible>
                                    </overrideCompatibilityChangeParameter>
                                </overrideCompatibilityChangeParameters>
                            </parameter>
                            <skip>${botmaker.japicmp.skip}</skip>
                        </configuration>
                    </plugin>
                </plugins>
            </build>
        </profile>

        <!--
          `pilot` profile: rebuild the BotPilot web client (Vite + React + TS in the sibling
          `botmaker-pilot` repository) from source and stage its `dist` as the assets this jar serves.

            mvn -Ppilot package

          The normal build needs no Node: a prebuilt `dist` is committed under src/main/resources/pilot/ and
          served as-is by internal/plugin/pilot/PilotServer. This profile refreshes those assets from source
          — it downloads a project-local Node/npm (nothing installed system-wide), runs `npm ci` +
          `npm run build`, then overwrites target/classes/pilot with the fresh dist (after the default
          resource copy, so the fresh build wins).

          It arrived here with the pilot on 2026-08-30. The client is served by the module that owns the
          server, which is this one: the pilot is a plugin's feature, and a plugin ships what its feature
          needs. Activated by id only, so an ordinary build — including JitPack's — never runs Node.
        -->
        <profile>
            <id>pilot</id>
            <properties>
                <pilot.web.dir>${project.basedir}/../botmaker-pilot/web</pilot.web.dir>
            </properties>
            <build>
                <plugins>
                    <plugin>
                        <groupId>com.github.eirslett</groupId>
                        <artifactId>frontend-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                        <version>1.15.1</version>
                        <configuration>
                            <workingDirectory>${pilot.web.dir}</workingDirectory>
                            <installDirectory>${project.build.directory}/node</installDirectory>
                        </configuration>
                        <executions>
                            <execution>
                                <id>install-node-npm</id>
                                <phase>generate-resources</phase>
                                <goals><goal>install-node-and-npm</goal></goals>
                                <configuration>
                                    <nodeVersion>v24.18.0</nodeVersion>
                                </configuration>
                            </execution>
                            <execution>
                                <id>npm-ci</id>
                                <phase>generate-resources</phase>
                                <goals><goal>npm</goal></goals>
                                <configuration>
                                    <arguments>ci</arguments>
                                </configuration>
                            </execution>
                            <execution>
                                <id>npm-build</id>
                                <phase>generate-resources</phase>
                                <goals><goal>npm</goal></goals>
                                <configuration>
                                    <arguments>run build</arguments>
                                </configuration>
                            </execution>
                        </executions>
                    </plugin>

                    <!-- Copy the freshly-built dist over the default-copied (committed) pilot assets in
                         target/classes, so a -Ppilot build serves the current source. -->
                    <plugin>
                        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                        <artifactId>maven-resources-plugin</artifactId>
                        <version>3.3.1</version>
                        <executions>
                            <execution>
                                <id>copy-pilot-dist</id>
                                <phase>process-classes</phase>
                                <goals><goal>copy-resources</goal></goals>
                                <configuration>
                                    <outputDirectory>${project.build.outputDirectory}/pilot</outputDirectory>
                                    <overwrite>true</overwrite>
                                    <resources>
                                        <resource>
                                            <directory>${pilot.web.dir}/dist</directory>
                                        </resource>
                                    </resources>
                                </configuration>
                            </execution>
                        </executions>
                    </plugin>
                </plugins>
            </build>
        </profile>
    </profiles>

</project>
